    "result_text": "The build succeeds and all tests pass. The change is complete.\n\n## Summary\n\nI moved the date-derived values to a fiscal-year basis (fiscal year starting 1 July, labeled `FY<YY>` by the calendar year it ends in), derived from the business-day-adjusted registration date.\n\n**`src/main/resources/openapi.yml`** (source for the generated `OwnerDto`):\n- Added a new read-only `fiscalYear` property (`FY<YY>`) to the `Owner` schema.\n- Updated `membershipNumber` and `membershipPoints` descriptions to reflect the fiscal-year basis.\n\n**`OwnerMapper.java`**:\n- Added `public static int fiscalYear(LocalDate)` \u2014 the single source of truth for the fiscal-year number (July\u2013December \u2192 next calendar year; January\u2013June \u2192 that year).\n- Added `String fiscalYear(Owner)` returning `FY<YY>` (or null when no registration date), wired into `toOwnerDto` via a `@Mapping` expression.\n- Changed tenure in `membershipPoints` from a 365-day window (`ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(...) > 365`) to elapsed fiscal years (`fiscalYear(now) - fiscalYear(registrationDate) > 1`).\n\n**`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**:\n- `membershipNumber(...)`'s year segment now uses `OwnerMapper.fiscalYear(registrationDate)` instead of the raw calendar year. Since the controller applies `toBusinessDay(...)` before storing, the registration date passed here is already business-day-adjusted.\n\nThe registration date is business-day-adjusted before both the mapper (`owner.getRegistrationDate()`) and the membership-number computation see it, so all fiscal-year-derived values use the adjusted date as required.\n\nThe suite is green (`Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 `AcceptanceTest.coreReturnsFiscalYear`), and the audit log confirms consistency: a registration date of `2026-09-07` yields membership number `...-M27` and would return `fiscalYear` `FY27`.\n\nNote: the spec fixed the format (`FY<YY>`) and the 1-July start but not the two-digit label convention; I used the end-year convention (e.g. 1 Jul 2026 \u2013 30 Jun 2027 \u2192 `FY27`), which the `FY\\d{2}` test accepts and which I documented consistently across the membership number, `fiscalYear`, and tenure logic.",
